.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.\"
.TH EXFAT-RESIZE 8 "2026-07-28" "exfat-resize @EXFAT_RESIZE_BUILD_VERSION@" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
exfat-resize \- grow or shrink an existing exFAT filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B exfat-resize
.I DEVICE
.RI [ SIZE ]
.br
.B exfat-resize
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.br
.B exfat-resize
.BR \-V " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B exfat-resize
grows or shrinks an existing exFAT filesystem in a regular file or raw block device.
For growth, enlarge the backing object first. For shrink, keep its original
size until the filesystem resize succeeds. The command does not resize regular
files or partitions or inspect partition tables.
.PP
The selected device must present the exFAT main boot sector at sector zero.
When
.I SIZE
is omitted, the filesystem grows to all space available in the backing object.
When it is supplied, the requested size is rounded down to a whole filesystem
sector. A smaller size requests shrink; an equal rounded size is rejected.
.PP
During the operation, the command reports each transaction stage.
After successful completion, it reports the resulting filesystem size in
bytes.
.PP
Shrink requires room below the new boundary for all current allocations,
including the full original allocation bitmap. The bitmap is shortened only
at final commit. This can reject exceptionally tight targets. After successful
shrink, a separate tool may truncate the image or reduce the partition end;
retain the partition start and at least the reported filesystem size.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I DEVICE
A regular file or raw block device containing the exFAT main boot sector at
sector zero.
Paths may resolve through symbolic links.
.TP
.I SIZE
The desired filesystem size, written as an unsigned decimal integer.
An optional uppercase
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
suffix multiplies the value by 1024, 1048576, or 1073741824, respectively.
Without a suffix, the value is interpreted as bytes.
The value must be greater than zero.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show concise command-line help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.SH SAFETY
.B WARNING:
Resizing modifies filesystem metadata in place and may result in data loss if
the operation fails or is interrupted.
Make and verify a backup before using this tool.
.PP
Verify that
.I DEVICE
resolves to the intended filesystem.
Before resizing, run an appropriate exFAT filesystem checker while the
filesystem is unmounted and ensure that the check completes successfully.
.B exfat-resize
is not a general filesystem checker; it validates only the invariants needed
for the resize.
.PP
Keep the filesystem unmounted and prevent all other access for the complete
operation.
When resizing a regular image file, also ensure that it is not attached through
a loop or disk-image device.
Platform locking helps detect cooperating users, but regular-file locks are
advisory and cannot exclude a process that ignores them.
.PP
A failed or interrupted resize is not automatically repaired, rolled back, or
resumable.
Follow the recovery guidance printed with an error.
.PP
Ctrl-C requests cooperative cancellation.
Work already in progress is allowed to reach the next safe boundary.
When the request is observed before completion, the command prints the
applicable recovery guidance, performs normal device cleanup, and exits with
status 130.
Further Ctrl-C requests remain handled during cleanup.
.PP
Every normal error exit prints stage-specific recovery guidance.
If the command terminates abnormally without printing stage-specific recovery
guidance, for example because of forced termination, a process crash, or power
loss, assume that destructive metadata updates may have started; do not retry
the resize, and restore the verified backup.
Follow a less conservative checker-and-retry path only when the command
explicitly reports that it is safe.
.PP
Shrink cancellation during compaction leaves a clean filesystem at its original
size when cleanup succeeds. Completed moves remain, and shrink may be retried.
Do not reduce backing storage after cancellation. Once the final geometry
commit begins, cancellation is deferred through completion.
.SH "FILESYSTEM COMPATIBILITY"
The filesystem must meet all of the following prerequisites:
.IP \(bu 2
Conform to exFAT revision 1.00 with a single FAT.
TexFAT and other two-FAT volumes are not supported.
.IP \(bu 2
Be clean and not have the exFAT media-failure flag set.
.IP \(bu 2
When accessed through a block device, have a filesystem sector size that is a
multiple of the block device's sector size.
.IP \(bu 2
Not contain Vendor Allocation directory entries, whose allocation semantics
cannot be interpreted without recognizing their vendor GUID.
.IP \(bu 2
For growth, gain enough clusters to hold the replacement allocation bitmap
in the newly added tail space.
.IP \(bu 2
For growth, not have a source cluster recorded as bad where the expanded FAT
would overlap it.
Bad clusters that remain in the Cluster Heap at the same physical location are
preserved.
.SH "RECOVERY AFTER FAILURE"
On failure,
.B exfat-resize
prints guidance for the recovery boundary reached:
.IP \(bu 2
If shrink cancellation left the original-size filesystem clean, use it or
retry shrink; do not reduce its backing storage.
.IP \(bu 2
If no filesystem write was attempted, correct the reported error and retry
when appropriate.
.IP \(bu 2
If the source filesystem remains authoritative, run an appropriate filesystem
checker before retrying.
.IP \(bu 2
If the filesystem may be incomplete, restore the verified backup.
.IP \(bu 2
If the resize completed but the filesystem's dirty state is uncertain, run an
appropriate filesystem checker and do not retry the resize.
.PP
If the command terminated abnormally without stage-specific guidance, follow
the conservative interruption procedure in
.BR SAFETY .
.SH "RESOURCE REQUIREMENTS"
Before writing,
.B exfat-resize
constructs and validates an in-memory allocation model for the target cluster
heap during growth, or the original heap during shrink.
Detailed allocator-backed working-memory requirements are documented in
.I docs/TRANSACTION.md
distributed with exfat-resize.
The CLI supplies those allocations with
.BR malloc (3)
and releases them with
.BR free (3),
so its working set is ordinary heap memory.
Peak heap use includes the 1 MiB I/O buffer, 768 KiB cache block, source-FAT
snapshot, four-byte-per-model-cluster allocation model, and directory worklist.
Shrink also retains relocation records for affected tail clusters and streams
and a directory identity index, allocated before the first write.
.SH EXAMPLES
Enlarge a regular image by 2 GiB, then grow its filesystem to use all available
space:
.PP
.RS
.nf
truncate \-s +2G image.exfat
exfat-resize image.exfat
.fi
.RE
.PP
Grow an unmounted filesystem on a raw block device to all available space:
.PP
.RS
.nf
exfat-resize /dev/your-exfat-device
.fi
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
The command exits with status zero after a successful resize or after showing
help or version information.
It exits with status 130 when Ctrl-C cancellation is observed.
It exits with another nonzero status when usage is invalid, the device cannot
be opened, validation fails, or the resize does not complete successfully.
.SH PLATFORMS
Linux and macOS are supported.
Raw block devices normally require privileges appropriate for the platform and
device.
.PP
The command uses platform synchronization barriers, but persistence ultimately
depends on the operating system and storage device honoring its flush requests.
.SH DOCUMENTATION
The
.I README.md
distributed with exfat-resize is the authoritative source for operational
safety requirements, supported-filesystem limitations, and recovery
instructions.
The current version is also available at:
.PP
.RS
https://github.com/huven/exfat-resize
.RE
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report issues at:
.PP
.RS
https://github.com/huven/exfat-resize/issues
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR fsck (8),
.BR fsck.exfat (8),
.BR fsck_exfat (8),
.BR mount (8),
.BR truncate (1)
